{
  "abstract": "We construct directed strongly regular graphs with parameters $(4r,2r-2,r-1,r-3,r-1)$ for every even integer $r\\geq8$, and a second family with parameters $(108+27s,33+9s,11+3s,8+3s,11+3s)$ for every $s\\geq0$. All positive integer multiples and complements also exist. Together these constructions supply fifteen complementary pairs marked unknown in the archived Hobart–Brouwer table, comprising thirty entries at orders from 32 to 108. The first family begins with $(32,14,7,5,7)$ and $(40,18,9,7,9)$. Its main mechanism is an induced extension of regular sign idempotents: a sign matrix $H$ with diagonal one, $H^2=qH$, both sums $q$, and a left-kernel sign vector admits a repeatable extension by $2q$ vertices. A local column imbalance is canceled exactly by the cross block. A ten-class certificate specifies the 32-vertex seed through a rank-two correction of its equitable quotient. Additional seeds at orders 48 and 80 attain the minimum possible number of outgoing neighborhoods, one more than the adjacency rank. A generalization using disjoint incoming patterns extends quotient certificates by several classes at once; a thirteen-class seed at order 108 permits a repeated 27-vertex extension. Complete proofs establish all seed identities, extensions, degree equations, and two-walk counts. Independent standard-library constructors reproduce every supplied matrix byte for byte, and exact path counting verifies all thirty graphs. Odd-rank members of the first parameter family are already known. The second extension requires the stated disjoint-pattern hypothesis; we do not classify all realizations or claim optimal neighborhood counts for the 32- and 40-vertex examples.",
